#module "memory"
'' std/memory: sharedptr -- shared ownership by reference count
''
'' SharedPtr( of T ) owns a heap object jointly with every other handle to it.
'' The object is destroyed exactly once, when the last SharedPtr to it goes
'' away -- at scope exit, on reassignment, or when a container holding it is
'' destroyed -- with no DELETE at the call site and no ownership convention to
'' document.  WeakPtr( of T ) observes the same object without keeping it alive,
'' which is the only way to break a cycle.
''
'' The idiom is not new here.  Channel( of T ) and Context both hand-rolled the
'' same copy-ctor / destructor / LET triad over a refcounted state block; this
'' is that, written once and generically.
''
'' --------------------------------------------------------------- the layout
''
'' Two allocations per object, not C++'s one: the control block and the object
'' are separate.  make_shared fuses them by embedding the object IN the control
'' block, which here would make the control block generic -- and because every
'' member of a generic is instantiated whether it is called or not, embedding
'' 'as T obj' would require T to be default-constructible for EVERY use of
'' SharedPtr, including the ones that adopt an already-built pointer.  A
'' non-generic control block holding 'any ptr' costs one more allocation and
'' keeps T unconstrained.  That is the trade, and it is deliberate.
''
'' SpCtl is a plain record with no members for the same reason it is not
'' generic: a control block with a virtual Dispose( ) would need a non-generic
'' base class whose method bodies live in this file, and as a header
'' (fb/sharedptr.bi) such a body was a duplicate symbol in every module that
'' included it.  So the control block stores no type information at all, and
'' DESTRUCTION IS DONE BY THE HANDLE, which knows T:  delete cptr( T ptr, ctl->obj ).
''
'' ---------------------------------------------------------------- the counts
''
'' 'strong' is the number of SharedPtr handles.  'weak' is the number of WeakPtr
'' handles PLUS ONE while strong > 0 -- the strong handles collectively hold one
'' weak reference between them.  So:
''
''   strong reaches 0  ->  the object is destroyed, and the collective weak
''                         reference is dropped
''   weak   reaches 0  ->  the control block itself is freed
''
'' which is why a WeakPtr can safely read the strong count of an object that has
'' already been destroyed: the block outlives the object.
''
'' ------------------------------------------------------------ thread safety
''
'' Under -mt every count is touched through the rtlib atomics in fbatomic.bi;
'' without -mt they are plain reads and writes.  The gate is __FB_MT__ and it is
'' safe because -mt is a whole-program link option -- a program cannot mix the
'' two, and without -mt it has no threads to race with.
''
'' THE GUARANTEE COVERS THE CONTROL BLOCK, NEVER THE POINTEE.  Two threads may
'' copy and destroy handles to the same object freely; two threads calling a
'' mutating member of the object itself still need their own lock.  This is
'' exactly C++'s contract.
''
'' ------------------------------------------------------ what is NOT provided
''
'' NO IMPLICIT DERIVED-TO-BASE CONVERSION.  A converting constructor would have
'' to be a member with its own type parameter -- 'declare constructor( of U )
'' ( byref rhs as SharedPtr( of U ) )' -- and members cannot have their own type
'' parameters.  Use SpCast( of Derived, Base )( sp ), which is explicit in both
'' types because only parameter positions are inferred.
''
'' SpCast REQUIRES A VIRTUAL DESTRUCTOR ON THE BASE.  C++ gets away without one
'' because its control block type-erases the deleter at construction; that needs
'' the address of an instantiated generic procedure, which is not expressible.
'' Here the surviving handle destroys through ITS OWN T, so destroying a Derived
'' through SharedPtr( of Base ) is correct only if Base's destructor is virtual.
'' There are no constraints on type parameters, so this cannot be enforced -- it
'' is a contract, and breaking it is silent.
''
'' NO UniquePtr.  The language has no move constructor, so a non-copyable owning
'' pointer could not be returned from a function.  DEFER covers the scope-local
'' case that would otherwise want one.
''
'' NO ALIASING CONSTRUCTOR, NO CUSTOM DELETERS, NO ARRAY FORM.  All three need
'' either type erasure of a deleter or a second stored pointer.
''
'' EVERY COPY IS A REAL REFCOUNT OPERATION.  There is no move constructor and no
'' return-value optimisation, so returning a SharedPtr by value costs an
'' increment and a decrement. Pass BYREF where it matters, as the containers do.
''
'' CYCLES STILL LEAK.  Two objects holding SharedPtrs to each other keep each
'' other alive for ever.  WeakPtr is the tool; it is not automatic.


'' For FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL, as std/outcome's optional.tiko does.
#include once "fberror.bi"

#ifdef __FB_MT__
    #include once "fbatomic.bi"
#endif

using FB

'' ---------------------------------------------------------------- the block
''
'' Deliberately non-generic, deliberately method-free.  See the header comment.
public type SpCtl
    as long    strong        '' SharedPtr handles
    as long    weak          '' WeakPtr handles, +1 while strong > 0
    as any ptr obj           '' the owned object; typed only by the handle
end type

'' ------------------------------------------------------- refcount primitives
''
'' All four return or test the value held BEFORE the operation, because "was I
'' the last one?" has to be answered by the same atomic step that does the
'' decrement.  Testing the count afterwards is the classic double-free.
''
'' Not 'public', so the module keeps them to itself. As a header they were PRIVATE so a header
'' included by two modules did not define them twice, as channel.bi's queue helpers were.

function hRefInc( byval p as long ptr ) as long
#ifdef __FB_MT__
    return AtomicFetchAddLong( p, 1 )
#else
    dim as long before = *p
    *p = before + 1
    return before
#endif
end function

function hRefDec( byval p as long ptr ) as long
#ifdef __FB_MT__
    return AtomicFetchAddLong( p, -1 )
#else
    dim as long before = *p
    *p = before - 1
    return before
#endif
end function

function hRefLoad( byval p as long ptr ) as long
#ifdef __FB_MT__
    return AtomicLoadLong( p )
#else
    return *p
#endif
end function

'' Increment only if the count is not already zero.
''
'' This is WeakPtr.Lock( ) and it is the one operation that genuinely needs
'' compare-and-swap: between reading a non-zero strong count and incrementing
'' it, the last SharedPtr may drop it to zero and destroy the object, so the
'' read and the increment have to be one step.  A plain increment here would
'' resurrect a destroyed object.
function hRefIncIfLive( byval p as long ptr ) as boolean
#ifdef __FB_MT__
    do
        dim as long cur = AtomicLoadLong( p )
        if( cur = 0 ) then
            return FALSE
        end if
        if( AtomicCasLong( p, cur, cur + 1 ) = cur ) then
            return TRUE
        end if
        '' another thread moved the count; re-read and try again
    loop
#else
    if( *p = 0 ) then
        return FALSE
    end if
    *p += 1
    return TRUE
#endif
end function

'' Drop one weak reference and free the block if it was the last.  The object
'' is already gone by the time this can reach zero, so there is nothing here
'' that needs to know T.
sub hWeakRelease( byval c as SpCtl ptr )
    if( c = nullptr ) then
        exit sub
    end if
    if( hRefDec( @c->weak ) = 1 ) then
        delete c
    end if
end sub

'' ------------------------------------------------------------------- handles

public type SharedPtr( of T )
    '' Internal.  Public only because SpCast and WeakPtr have to reach it;
    '' there is no friendship in the language.  Do not touch it.
    as SpCtl ptr ctl

    declare constructor( )
    declare constructor( byval p as T ptr )
    declare constructor( byref rhs as SharedPtr( of T ) )
    declare destructor( )
    declare operator let( byref rhs as SharedPtr( of T ) )

    declare function Get( ) as T ptr
    declare function Ref( ) byref as T
    declare function IsNull( ) as boolean
    declare function UseCount( ) as long
    declare sub Reset( )

    '' internal: take a block that has ALREADY been counted for this handle
    declare sub hAdopt( byval c as SpCtl ptr )
    '' internal: drop this handle's strong reference, destroying if last
    declare sub hRelease( )
end type

public type WeakPtr( of T )
    as SpCtl ptr ctl

    declare constructor( )
    declare constructor( byref rhs as SharedPtr( of T ) )
    declare constructor( byref rhs as WeakPtr( of T ) )
    declare destructor( )
    declare operator let( byref rhs as WeakPtr( of T ) )
    declare operator let( byref rhs as SharedPtr( of T ) )

    declare function Lock( ) as SharedPtr( of T )
    declare function Expired( ) as boolean
    declare function UseCount( ) as long
    declare sub Reset( )
end type

'' -------------------------------------------------------------- SharedPtr

constructor SharedPtr( of T )( )
    this.ctl = nullptr
end constructor

'' Adopts a raw pointer.  The SharedPtr owns it from here and will DELETE it;
'' handing the same raw pointer to two SharedPtrs gives two owners and a double
'' free, exactly as in C++.  MakeShared( ) avoids the question.
constructor SharedPtr( of T )( byval p as T ptr )
    if( p = nullptr ) then
        this.ctl = nullptr
        exit constructor
    end if
    dim as SpCtl ptr c = new SpCtl
    c->strong = 1
    c->weak   = 1
    c->obj    = p
    this.ctl  = c
end constructor

'' BYREF, and it has to be: a constructor taking its own type BYVAL is rejected
'' outright (a copy constructor that copies to call itself cannot terminate).
constructor SharedPtr( of T )( byref rhs as SharedPtr( of T ) )
    this.ctl = rhs.ctl
    if( this.ctl ) then
        hRefInc( @this.ctl->strong )
    end if
end constructor

destructor SharedPtr( of T )( )
    this.hRelease( )
end destructor

'' Release the old target BEFORE taking the new one, and do nothing at all when
'' they are the same block.  Both halves matter: without the early-out, 'sp = sp'
'' releases the last reference and destroys the object it is about to re-take;
'' without the ordering, assigning a handle into a container slot that holds the
'' only other reference destroys the object while the right-hand side still
'' names it.
operator SharedPtr( of T ).let( byref rhs as SharedPtr( of T ) )
    if( this.ctl = rhs.ctl ) then
        exit operator
    end if
    this.hRelease( )
    this.ctl = rhs.ctl
    if( this.ctl ) then
        hRefInc( @this.ctl->strong )
    end if
end operator

sub SharedPtr( of T ).hAdopt( byval c as SpCtl ptr )
    this.ctl = c
end sub

'' The only place the object is destroyed, and the only member that needs to
'' know T at all.
sub SharedPtr( of T ).hRelease( )
    dim as SpCtl ptr c = this.ctl
    this.ctl = nullptr
    if( c = nullptr ) then
        exit sub
    end if
    if( hRefDec( @c->strong ) = 1 ) then
        '' last strong reference: destroy the object, then drop the single
        '' weak reference that all the strong handles shared
        delete cptr( T ptr, c->obj )
        c->obj = nullptr
        hWeakRelease( c )
    end if
end sub

sub SharedPtr( of T ).Reset( )
    this.hRelease( )
end sub

function SharedPtr( of T ).Get( ) as T ptr
    if( this.ctl = nullptr ) then
        return nullptr
    end if
    return cptr( T ptr, this.ctl->obj )
end function

'' Raises 'illegal function call' when null.
''
'' Unlike Optional.Value( ), there is no stored slot to fall back on: a null
'' SharedPtr owns nothing, so there is no T anywhere to return a reference to.
'' A function-static blank would be worse than the fault -- it would require T
'' to be default-constructible for every use of SharedPtr, and a function-static
'' UDT is constructed lazily and NEVER destroyed, so it would retain one T and
'' its resources for the life of the program.  So: the error is raised, and if
'' an 'on error' handler returns control the dereference below faults.  Reading
'' through a null handle is a bug at the call site.
function SharedPtr( of T ).Ref( ) byref as T
    if( this.ctl = nullptr ) then
        '' QUALIFIED, and it has to be.  A generic body is replayed at the
        '' INSTANTIATION site and its names resolve there, not here -- so an
        '' unqualified FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL made this usable only
        '' from a scope that had already said `using FB`, and failed with
        '' `error 42: Variable not declared` everywhere else.  Same defect and
        '' same fix as result.bi; they were not corrected together.
        error( FB.FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
    end if
    return *cptr( T ptr, this.ctl->obj )
end function

function SharedPtr( of T ).IsNull( ) as boolean
    return this.ctl = nullptr
end function

'' Advisory under -mt: another thread may add or drop a handle before you act on
'' the answer.  UseCount( ) = 1 is the only reading that is stable, and only
'' because no other thread can be holding one.
function SharedPtr( of T ).UseCount( ) as long
    if( this.ctl = nullptr ) then
        return 0
    end if
    return hRefLoad( @this.ctl->strong )
end function

'' --------------------------------------------------------------- WeakPtr

constructor WeakPtr( of T )( )
    this.ctl = nullptr
end constructor

constructor WeakPtr( of T )( byref rhs as SharedPtr( of T ) )
    this.ctl = rhs.ctl
    if( this.ctl ) then
        hRefInc( @this.ctl->weak )
    end if
end constructor

constructor WeakPtr( of T )( byref rhs as WeakPtr( of T ) )
    this.ctl = rhs.ctl
    if( this.ctl ) then
        hRefInc( @this.ctl->weak )
    end if
end constructor

destructor WeakPtr( of T )( )
    hWeakRelease( this.ctl )
    this.ctl = nullptr
end destructor

operator WeakPtr( of T ).let( byref rhs as WeakPtr( of T ) )
    if( this.ctl = rhs.ctl ) then
        exit operator
    end if
    hWeakRelease( this.ctl )
    this.ctl = rhs.ctl
    if( this.ctl ) then
        hRefInc( @this.ctl->weak )
    end if
end operator

operator WeakPtr( of T ).let( byref rhs as SharedPtr( of T ) )
    if( this.ctl = rhs.ctl ) then
        exit operator
    end if
    hWeakRelease( this.ctl )
    this.ctl = rhs.ctl
    if( this.ctl ) then
        hRefInc( @this.ctl->weak )
    end if
end operator

sub WeakPtr( of T ).Reset( )
    hWeakRelease( this.ctl )
    this.ctl = nullptr
end sub

'' Returns a null SharedPtr if the object is already gone.
''
'' The increment-if-live is a single atomic step; see hRefIncIfLive.  On success
'' this handle has taken a strong reference which is handed to 'r' WITHOUT a
'' second increment -- hAdopt exists for exactly that.
function WeakPtr( of T ).Lock( ) as SharedPtr( of T )
    dim as SharedPtr( of T ) r
    if( this.ctl = nullptr ) then
        return r
    end if
    if( hRefIncIfLive( @this.ctl->strong ) ) then
        r.hAdopt( this.ctl )
    end if
    return r
end function

function WeakPtr( of T ).Expired( ) as boolean
    if( this.ctl = nullptr ) then
        return TRUE
    end if
    return hRefLoad( @this.ctl->strong ) = 0
end function

function WeakPtr( of T ).UseCount( ) as long
    if( this.ctl = nullptr ) then
        return 0
    end if
    return hRefLoad( @this.ctl->strong )
end function

'' -------------------------------------------------------------- operators
''
'' Global, one parameter: a non-self operator cannot be a method.  T is inferred
'' through the nested SharedPtr( of T ) position.
''
'' Both return BYREF, so '*sp' and 'sp->field' are lvalues and a write through
'' them reaches the object rather than a copy of it.
''
'' '->' additionally requires T to be a UDT -- the compiler requires the result
'' of operator -> to be a structure, since it chains member access through it.
'' 'SharedPtr( of integer )' is fine; writing 'sp->' on one is a compile error,
'' and '*sp' or sp.Ref( ) is the way to reach a scalar.
''
'' Neither checks for null: they compile to the same dereference the equivalent
'' raw pointer would, and adding a branch to every field access to catch a bug
'' the call site already has is the wrong trade.  Use Ref( ) for the checked
'' form.

public operator * ( of T )( byref p as SharedPtr( of T ) ) byref as T
    return *cptr( T ptr, p.ctl->obj )
end operator

public operator -> ( of T )( byref p as SharedPtr( of T ) ) byref as T
    return *cptr( T ptr, p.ctl->obj )
end operator

'' ------------------------------------------------------------- construction
''
'' Free procedures, and separately named rather than overloaded, because a
'' generic procedure cannot be overloaded: one name, one generic procedure.

'' Allocates a default-constructed T.  Requires T to be default-constructible --
'' which costs nothing, because this is a free procedure and is only
'' instantiated for the T's it is actually called with.
public function MakeShared( of T )( ) as SharedPtr( of T )
    dim as SpCtl ptr c = new SpCtl
    c->strong = 1
    c->weak   = 1
    c->obj    = new T

    dim as SharedPtr( of T ) r
    r.hAdopt( c )
    return r
end function

'' Allocates a copy of an existing value.
''
'' 'new T( v )' copy-constructs, which works for scalars, for plain records, and
'' for any T with an explicit copy constructor.  It does NOT work for a T that
'' declares some other constructor but no copy constructor -- the compiler
'' resolves the initialiser against the declared constructors only.  Build that
'' one yourself and hand the pointer to SharedPtr( of T )( p ).
public function MakeSharedFrom( of T )( byref v as T ) as SharedPtr( of T )
    dim as SpCtl ptr c = new SpCtl
    c->strong = 1
    c->weak   = 1
    c->obj    = new T( v )

    dim as SharedPtr( of T ) r
    r.hAdopt( c )
    return r
end function

'' Derived-to-base, explicit in both types.
''
''     dim as SharedPtr( of Shape ) s = SpCast( of Circle, Shape )( c )
''
'' Both type arguments have to be written: inference reads parameter positions
'' only, and B appears nowhere in the parameter list.
''
'' Safe to reinterpret because SpCtl is not generic -- one layout for every T --
'' and because FreeBASIC is single-inheritance, so a Derived pointer and its
'' Base subobject share an address and there is no adjustment to apply.
''
'' BASE MUST HAVE A VIRTUAL DESTRUCTOR.  The returned handle destroys through
'' 'delete cptr( B ptr, obj )'; without a virtual destructor that runs Base's
'' destructor on a Derived object.  Nothing here can check it.
public function SpCast( of D, B )( byref s as SharedPtr( of D ) ) as SharedPtr( of B )
    dim as SharedPtr( of B ) r
    if( s.ctl = nullptr ) then
        return r
    end if
    hRefInc( @s.ctl->strong )
    r.hAdopt( s.ctl )
    return r
end function

